Oz: the prediction rounds the player's velocity as the server's game does (cg_predict.c CG_OzGridSnap, bg_pmove.c
bg_gridSnap). id's game rounds it to whole units after every command, which makes its movement depend on the frame
rate: at 1,000 fps a jump falls with a gravity of 1000, at 125 with 750. Area 51's servers (2026-11-10, read from
their snapshots with cl_ozProbe 4 at 125, 250, 500 and 1,000 fps: pmove_fixed 0 in the systeminfo, sv_fps 40) move a
player above 125 fps another way: his velocity is rounded only at the 8 ms marks of the server's clock, so he falls
and jumps as 125 fps does (a 48-unit jump at every frame rate), and between marks a snapshot shows the speed
unrounded (whole at the last mark, less the gravity of the milliseconds since). At 125 fps they round after every
command, as id's. Oz's cgame predicted id's way there: no miss at 125 fps, a miss at most snapshots while moving
above it. The cgame now tells the two ways apart from the snapshots (in the air, and running; cg_ozGridSnap -1; 0
id's way, 1 the marks') and predicts with the server's: a move is cut at the marks, and only a piece that ends on
one rounds. A fall that ends within a quarter unit of a level floor just lands there (no overbounce, as Area 51's
snapshots show): cut at the marks, half the predicted landings at 500 fps bounced, a 17 unit miss each.

Live on Area 51's Duel behind 48 ms (cg_showmiss): at 500 fps strafe jumps missed at 64 and 76 of 96 snapshots id's
way, 1 and 1 this way; running 25 and 7, then 0; at 1,000 fps a walk with jumps and strafes 266 of 428, then 10 of
469 (those left were the bot's hits). Oz's own servers move in fixed 8 ms steps (0022); g_ozGridSnap 1 with
pmove_fixed 0 makes the game move this way too, for server_cvars_test.py's stand-in (176 misses id's way, 2 this
way). cg_showmiss 3 prints each miss's direction and both speeds.

